  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">FROM CONFLICT TO COEXISTENCE: CONSERVING AFRICAN ELEPHANTS IN TANZANIA&apos;S GREATER MKOMAZI LANDSCAPE</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE PROJECT FROM CONFLICT TO COEXISTENCE CONSERVING AFRICAN ELEPHANTS IN TANZANIAS GREATER MKOMAZI LANDSCAPE ADDRESSES ESCALATING HUMAN ELEPHANT CONFLICT IN THE TSAVO MKOMAZI ECOSYSTEM ONE OF THE LARGEST PROTECTED TRANSBOUNDARY ECOSYSTEMS IN EAST AFRICA. CONNECTIVITY BETWEEN MKOMAZI NATIONAL PARK AND TSAVO WEST NATIONAL PARK IS CRUCIAL FOR ELEPHANTS AND A RANGE OF OTHER SPECIES THAT DISPERSE SEASONALLY IN SEARCH OF FOOD WATER AND BREEDING GROUNDS. AS HUMAN AND WILDLIFE POPULATIONS HAVE INCREASED IN THIS LANDSCAPE THERE IS A NEED TO IDENTIFY EVIDENCE BASED SOLUTIONS THAT REDUCE CONFLICT AND BALANCE THE NEEDS OF PEOPLE AND WILDLIFE. AFRICAN PEOPLE WILDLIFE IN PARTNERSHIP WITH LOCAL COMMUNITIES GOVERNMENT AND NON PROFIT STAKEHOLDERS WILL COLLECT DATA ON ELEPHANT MOVEMENT MONITOR AND EVALUATE HUMAN ELEPHANT CONFLICT IMPLEMENT HUMAN ELEPHANT COEXISTENCE STRATEGIES AND STRENGTHEN CROSS BOUNDARY PARTNERSHIPS FOR CONSERVATION. TO BETTER UNDERSTAND WHERE WHEN AND WHY ELEPHANTS ARE MOVING ACROSS THE LANDSCAPE BOTH WITHIN AND BEYOND THE NATIONAL PARK 12 ELEPHANTS WILL BE COLLARED AND MONITORED OVER THE COURSE OF THREE YEARS. BUILDING UPON APWS SUCCESSFUL CONFLICT RESOLUTION MODELS WE WILL DEPLOY HUMAN ELEPHANT COEXISTENCE OFFICERS TO ENGAGE WITH COMMUNITIES GATHER AND EVALUATE CONFLICT DATA AND IMPLEMENT MITIGATION STRAT</narrative>
  </description>
